WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.260
How does the shortest-link TSP

2
00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:05.420
heuristic differ from Kruskal? We

3
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.000
will scan edges in global

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.380
weight order, enforce both degree

5
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.680
and cycle restrictions, and close

6
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.340
a spanning path into a

7
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:13.600
tour.

8
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.280
Unlike nearest neighbour, shortest link

9
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.640
considers the globally lightest remaining

10
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.740
edge rather than only edges

11
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.280
from one current vertex. Accept

12
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.080
it only if neither endpoint

13
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.300
would exceed degree two and

14
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.420
it would not create a

15
00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.380
premature cycle.

16
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.780
Use five vertices with the

17
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.380
first six sorted edges A

18
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.020
B one, B C two,

19
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.340
A C three, B D

20
00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.640
four, C D five and

21
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.200
D E six. The remaining

22
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.800
edges are A E seven,

23
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.700
B E eight, A D

24
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.080
nine and C E ten,

25
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.140
completing the graph.

26
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.520
Accept A B because both

27
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.660
endpoints have degree zero and

28
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.780
no cycle is possible. Then

29
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.320
accept B C, leaving B

30
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.060
with degree two and A

31
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.700
and C with degree one.

32
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.160
Next consider A C, which

33
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.260
would close the three-vertex cycle

34
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.100
A B C A. Reject

35
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.620
it even though the endpoint

36
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.300
degrees would remain at most

37
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.480
two, because D and E

38
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.860
have not yet joined the

39
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.140
tour.

40
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.140
Next consider B D, which

41
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.360
would give B a third

42
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.800
incident accepted edge. Reject it

43
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.520
for the degree rule, even

44
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.860
though connecting D would not

45
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:23.780
create a cycle.

46
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.380
C D passes both tests,

47
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.980
so accept it and extend

48
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.960
the current path. Then D

49
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.840
E also passes, giving the

50
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.940
spanning path A, B, C,

51
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.940
D, E with four accepted

52
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.360
edges.

53
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.880
A path spanning all five

54
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.500
vertices has exactly two degree-one

55
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.160
endpoints, A and E. Add

56
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.880
their connecting edge A E

57
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.140
to form the final tour;

58
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.300
this is the permitted final

59
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.900
cycle, not a premature subtour.

60
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.660
The total tour cost is

61
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.080
one plus two plus five

62
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.920
plus six plus seven, which

63
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.780
equals twenty-one. This trace demonstrates

64
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.780
the rules and feasibility; it

65
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.080
does not prove shortest link

66
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.040
always finds an optimal TSP

67
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:05.400
tour.

68
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.580
Kruskal also scans edges globally

69
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.940
and rejects cycles, but it

70
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.000
permits vertices of degree greater

71
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.880
than two. Its goal is

72
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.200
a spanning tree, whereas shortest

73
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.500
link must maintain path-like pieces

74
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.060
and eventually create one Hamiltonian

75
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:22.480
cycle.

76
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.200
Sorting E edges costs big

77
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.800
O of E log E,

78
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.340
and union-find plus degree counters

79
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.980
can check the scan efficiently.

80
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.100
With suitable union-find operations, the

81
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.840
scan contributes big O of

82
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.720
E times inverse Ackermann of

83
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.860
V, alongside the sorting cost.

84
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.240
In a complete V-vertex graph,

85
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.220
E is order V squared,

86
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.240
so edge storage and sorting

87
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.500
can be substantial. State tie

88
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.180
rules if equal weights occur,

89
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:51.780
and do not claim this

90
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.980
heuristic dominates nearest neighbour on

91
00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.840
every input just because it

92
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.200
does better in one example.

93
00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.600
Things to take note of:

94
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.540
track endpoint degrees and connected

95
00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.420
components separately, because each rejects

96
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.840
a different kind of invalid

97
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.400
choice. Stop after the spanning

98
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.600
path, include the final closing

99
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.940
edge, and distinguish a TSP

100
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.960
tour from an MST before

101
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.900
borrowing Kruskal's explanation, hmph.
